\section{Using the figure table}{

This CSV is a list of \strong{figure metadata}, not a collection of image files. Enter one row for each entire figure or each lettered part, giving its \code{figure_number} and, when applicable, \code{figure_part}. The \code{taxon} and \code{specimen_code} columns are required to exist but may be blank for a map or other overview figure.

Add your own useful columns, such as \code{feature}, \code{angle}, \code{tag} or \code{specimen_category}. Later, figure filters use those column names and values to find the correct illustration; they do not require a standard plate arrangement. Keep specimen codes consistent with the character-state matrix if descriptions must cite a particular specimen.

